Langmuir and Freundlich adsorption isotherm models are expressed in Eqs. (1) and (2), respectively59. According to Eqs. (1) and (2), the maximal adsorption capacity (Qm) was fitted when the initial U(VI) concentration was 5–150 mg L−1 (Fig. 5). The calculated Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm parameters from the fitting processes are listed in Table 1. Table 1 shows that Langmuir isotherm fitted the experimental data well with a higher correlation coefficient (R2), and the maximum adsorption capacity was 241.28 mg g−1 for the adsorption of HCF. Moreover, Fig. 5 shows that HCF is a promising sorbent for the removal of uranium from aqueous solutions in terms of the preparation cost, adsorption efficiency and simple solid–liquid separation for uranium from aqueous solutions.1\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
